= Deductive reasoning =

== Overview ==
'''Deductive reasoning''' aims at conclusions that follow necessarily from premises under a specified consequence relation.

A deductively valid argument cannot have all true premises and a false conclusion.

== Validity and soundness ==
Validity is structural.

For example:

<pre>
If P, then Q.
P.
Therefore Q.
</pre>

is valid regardless of the particular content substituted for P and Q.

If the premises are also true, the argument is sound.

Therefore:

<pre>
validity ≠ truth
validity ≠ soundness
</pre>

== Deduction and certainty ==
Deductive validity is not the same as psychological certainty.

A person can be highly confident in an invalid argument or uncertain about a valid one.

Deduction concerns consequence, not subjective confidence.

== Deduction and other reasoning ==
[[Inductive reasoning]] supports conclusions without guaranteeing them.

[[Abductive reasoning]] uses explanatory considerations.

Deduction differs because the conclusion is constrained by what follows from the premises under the logic used.

== Human reasoning ==
Psychological studies investigate how people solve syllogisms, conditionals, and other deductive tasks.

Performance depends on representation, language, prior knowledge, working memory, and task interpretation.

Formal validity and human performance therefore belong to related but distinct levels of analysis.

